# Metadata and comments

## Metadata

@code(language="text")
@metadata
title = Project Log
author = Subbu
date = 2026-08-06
tags = parser, format
@endmetadata
@endcode

One `key = value` per line. Keys are case-insensitive and trimmed. A document
may contain at most one metadata block; a second is a warning.

Readers surface it as document properties rather than rendering it inline, so
it never appears in the body. `xtxt export html` uses `title` for the page
title, falling back to the first heading.

It is returned by `xtxt extract` under `metadata`.

@note
Title: Not frontmatter
There is no special syntax at the top of the file. Metadata is an ordinary
directive that happens to be conventionally placed first, which means it
follows the same rules as everything else and needs no separate parser.
@endnote

## Comments

@code(language="text")
@comment
Reminder: check these numbers against the Q3 export before publishing.
@endcomment
@endcode

Never rendered, in any output format. The payload is taken literally, so it can
contain anything.

In VS Code, the block-comment shortcut wraps a selection in `@comment`.

## Escaping

A backslash makes the next character literal:

@code(language="text")
\*not emphasis\*
\[not a link\](x)
@endcode

Escaping is only needed where a mark would otherwise be read as syntax. Inside
`@code`, `@math`, `@mermaid`, `@raw` and `@comment`, nothing is interpreted and
no escaping is required.

## Horizontal rule

@code(language="text")
@hr
@endcode
